WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.890
In lab TD6, we assign 8 separate programs
to act as concurrent ticket sellers for a

2
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.440
shared pool of 100,000 tickets.

3
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.820
But when we check this terminal
output, the math breaks.

4
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:21.440
They sold 103,195 tickets, leaving
a negative balance of minus 7.

5
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.260
The code executed exactly as written.

6
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.880
The sellers are simply
threads in a C process.

7
00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:30.970
You can see the subtraction command,
tickets--, running right here inside

8
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.890
a while loop. How can a computer accurately
run a subtraction command, yet sell more

9
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.820
items than actually exist?

10
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.820
The answer lies in how threads execute.

11
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.910
The illusion of simultaneous execution
creates hidden blind spots that silently

12
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.140
corrupt your data. Developers use threads
instead of full processes for one reason,

13
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.830
speed. As this output shows, creating a
process on our course machine takes 248.8

14
00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.980
microseconds. That costs 6.0 times
more than creating a single thread.

15
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.940
Threads are faster because
they don't copy data.

16
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.720
Architecturally, user mode sits above the
privilege boundary, kernel mode below.

17
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.040
Inside a user process,
threads operate together.

18
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:19.000
They share the whole address space,
code, descriptors, heap, and global data.

19
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.240
But each thread maintains its own
private stack and hardware registers.

20
00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:28.100
This shared memory architecture is
fast, but it is physically vulnerable.

21
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:32.210
Because there is no memory protection
between them, a bug in one thread can

22
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.380
instantly corrupt everyone's data.

23
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.450
Let's look back at that specific line of
C code responsible for the ticket sale

24
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:44.430
subtraction. For an x86-64 processor,
a single line of C code is not a single

25
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.340
atomic step. The hardware breaks the
subtraction down into three distinct steps,

26
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.690
reading the variable from memory into a
register, subtracting one, and writing the

27
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.970
new value back to memory. The problem
occurs when two separate threads attempt to

28
00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.220
run these steps at the exact same time.

29
00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.770
If thread A and thread B perform the read
step simultaneously, they both pull the

30
00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.960
exact same starting number from memory.

31
00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.010
Both threads then subtract one in their
private registers, and both write the exact

32
00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.900
same updated number back to shared memory.

33
00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.620
One write completely overwrites the other.

34
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.660
This temporal collision
is a race condition.

35
00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.710
Because the scheduler can interleave these
hardware steps in any order, basic read

36
00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.480
and write operations are
unsafe for shared data.

37
00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.550
To fix this, we need a critical section,
a protected fragment of code that only one

38
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.740
thread may run at a time to
ensure mutual exclusion.

39
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:42.140
The standard tool to enforce this boundary
is the POSIX function pthread_mutex_lock.

40
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.530
As this code shows, whoever takes the
lock executes their three-step operation

41
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:51.720
uninterrupted. If another thread arrives,
it sleeps until the mutex is unlocked.

42
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.900
Running this protected code
yields the correct result.

43
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.970
The output now accurately records exactly
100,000 tickets sold, with no lost

44
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:04.040
updates. A mutex acts as a
strict physical bottleneck.

45
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.120
It trades maximum parallel speed
for guaranteed data integrity.

46
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.780
There is a structurally different
alternative to locking, the semaphore.

47
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.330
A semaphore is an integer equipped with
inherently atomic operations built into the

48
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.890
hardware. Instead of locking a separate
section of code to update a variable, the

49
00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.360
semaphore actually is the ticket counter.

50
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.970
A function like sem_trywait checks the
integer and subtracts one in a single

51
00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:33.410
indivisible step. Utilizing hardware-level
atomic instructions completely

52
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.740
eliminates the intermediate states
where race conditions thrive.

53
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.070
Let's review. What memory does a thread
share with other threads in the same

54
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.380
process, and what is kept private?

55
00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.110
They share code, global data, the heap,
and descriptors, but each has its own

56
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.940
private stack and registers.

57
00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:55.540
Question two, why is subtracting a shared
variable in C not an atomic operation?

58
00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.290
Because the processor executes it in three
separate steps, reading, subtracting, and

59
00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:07.800
writing. Now open animations A7.1 and
A7.4 in the course hub and run lab TD6.
